x87 shadow doubles: keep the FPU stack as host doubles across instructions.

Patch 05 does the common x87 case on the host FPU inside the helpers; the
cost that remains is structural: a call per instruction and an 80-bit
round trip through env->fpregs[]. This patch adds eight scalar binary64
opcodes to TCG (add/sub/mul/div/fmsub/sqrt/cvt_f64_f32/cvt_f32_f64 on
raw bit patterns in i64 temps; VEX scalar SSE + FMA3 on x86-64, scalar
FP on aarch64) and a translator mode (target/i386/tcg/x87-shadow.c.inc)
that, at PC=53 or PC=24 / RC=nearest / PE masked (2-bit TB flag from
env->x87_fast_mode), keeps every x87 register it touches as a double in a TCG global and
converts back to the 80-bit form only at TB exits, before helpers, and
on exceptions (an insn_start word + x86_restore_state_to_opc repair).
The stack is addressed relative to the entry top so no TB flag bits are
spent on it; push/pop keep env->fpstt/fptags current. Inlined: fld/fst/
fstp m32/m64, fild/fist/fistp m16/m32, the six arithmetic ops in every
form incl. popping, fld/fst/fstp st(i), fxch, fcmov, fld1/fldz, fchs/
fabs, fsqrt, frndint, fcom/fucom/fcomi families, ftst, ffree, fincstp/
fdecstp, fnstsw ax. Anything the fast path cannot take (NaN, inf,
denormal, 64-bit mantissa, out-of-window, div by zero, underflow, float
overflow, integer overflow) branches to an out-of-line slow block that
writes everything back, runs the unmodified helper sequence and exits
the TB, so results, tags and the status word equal softfloat's. One
deliberate deviation: a popped register keeps its previous fpregs[]
content rather than the popped value (only observable by reading an
empty register, which QEMU never faults on).

Measured on a Ryzen 7 5700X, DOS 7-op loop (ns per x87 op): softfloat
21.6, patch 05 helpers 10.6, patch 06 2.9. tools/x87-guest-test.py
(68 op sequences x 44 operands^2 x 7 control words): 382,250 result
lines identical with the fast path on and off. A linux-user SIGSEGV test
with two dirty shadows verified the unwind repair. 
The aarch64 backend also needed a fix upstream never exercised: tcg_out_mov
vector->general emitted UMOV with a zero element-size field (SIGILL).

PC=24 (what Direct3D sets) uses the same shadows: they hold values with a
24-bit significand inside the float exponent window, and every result is
rounded through a binary32 conversion (correct for 24-bit operands, since
53 >= 2*24+2). m64 operands round to 24 bits with PE, as softfloat does;
an fild m32 wider than 24 bits takes the helper. Same guest test, all
seven control words identical on/off.
